A mutex is a synchronization mechanism used in computer programming to ensure that only one thread or process can access a particular resource at a time. It is a simple yet powerful tool that helps prevent race conditions and deadlocks in concurrent programming.

When a thread wants to access a shared resource, it first acquires a lock on that resource. The lock is a special piece of memory that is used to control access to the resource. When a thread acquires a lock, it becomes the owner of the lock and can access the resource. Other threads that try to acquire the same lock will be blocked until the first thread releases the lock.

Mutexes are commonly used in multi-threaded applications to ensure that only one thread can modify a shared variable or perform a critical operation at a time. This prevents data corruption and ensures that the application behaves predictably.

Here is an example of how a mutex can be used in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>

std::mutex mtx; // mutex for critical section

void print_number(int num) {
    mtx.lock(); // acquire lock
    std::cout << "Thread " << std::this_thread::get_id() << " is printing " << num << std::endl;
    mtx.unlock(); // release lock
}
```

In this example, the `print_number` function is a critical section that modifies a shared variable. To ensure that only one thread can execute this function at a time, a mutex is used to acquire a lock before entering the critical section and release the lock after exiting the critical section. This prevents other threads from entering the critical section while the first thread is executing it.
